From 3576553226a71a46baafe6f3532e6a2d6d65748c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:31:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 94/97] test: check that every demuxer hands the same span over
 twice

An item that repeats in place is seeked back to its start with the clock left
running, so a demuxer that gives up a little of its content at that seek makes
no audible seam: the next pass simply begins where the last one stopped. What
is lost is a sliver that is never played, and the drift it leaves gathers loop
after loop until the output's correction saturates and the tone detunes, by
which point nothing points back at the demuxer. So measure the demuxer alone.

Each sample is opened with a recording es_out modelled on the one in
demux-run.c, demuxed to the end while noting, per elementary stream, the date
on the first block and the furthest point its data reaches, seeked back to the
start the way a repeat does, and demuxed to the end again. Those two numbers
must come out the same both times. There is no clock in that, no audio output
and no decoder, so it says the same thing on any machine.

The samples are a second of a 440 Hz tone, one container each and 124 KB for
all fifteen, and each is handed to a named demuxer rather than to whichever
module wins the probe, so the coverage does not quietly shrink when a tree is
configured differently. wav, aiff, au, voc, tta, mp4, matroska, avi, asf, ts
and mpeg-ps repeat their spans exactly. Four do not: flacsys hands over every
block of the repeated pass with no length at all, so the item reads 24762 us
short of where its data really ends; the mpeg audio es demuxer resumes a whole
72 ms frame late; ogg gives back nothing at all on the second pass for vorbis
and restarts most of a second late for opus; and avformat drops its first
frame. Those are left failing on purpose - the test exists to say so.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
